// Source-reviewed Module 2 Lessons 9–12; bundled locally for offline use.
(function(){window.BE=window.BE||{};window.BE.expressionCourseContent=Object.assign(window.BE.expressionCourseContent||{},{"m2-l9":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed and internally reviewed teaching-content draft. It is based on <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, printed p.256, with the conditional-boundary cross-reference to p.260 and the accepted C09 teaching flow. The source-specific meanings of can, ought and shall are kept distinct. The fixed group be able to reinforces only the ability meaning. All 25 closed practice prompts have matching answers. Actual learner time and user feedback remain unmeasured; the lesson is not user-tested or App-integrated.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>The source treats <strong>can</strong>, <strong>ought</strong> and <strong>shall</strong> as ordinary-English words that need special handling when we express an action in Basic English. They are not entries in the 850-word list; the lesson teaches the Basic constructions the source gives for their different meanings. They are not three words with one fixed Chinese meaning each.</p><p>The learner must identify the relation expressed:</p><ol><li>ability — a person is able to do something;</li><li>possibility — it is possible for the person to do it;</li><li>advisability — it is right or wise to do it;</li><li>duty or responsibility — it is the person&#39;s business to do it;</li><li>probability — the fact supports an expectation;</li><li>intention or decision — the speaker asks whether they are going to do something.</li></ol><p>The question is not simply “Which modal verb is this?” It is “What does the speaker mean about the action?”</p><h3>1. can — ability or possibility</h3><p>The source gives two expressions:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Meaning of can</th><th>Basic expression in the source</th><th>What it describes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I can do it — I have the ability</td><td>I am able to do it</td><td>The person&#39;s ability or capacity</td></tr><tr><td>I can do it — it is possible for me</td><td>It is possible for me to do it</td><td>The possibility of doing it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not treat these as identical:</p><ul><li><strong>be able to do it</strong> describes ability;</li><li><strong>it is possible for me to do it</strong> describes possibility.</li></ul><p>The same ordinary-English sentence may need context before we know which relation is intended. Do not choose one expression merely because the word can appears.</p><h3>2. ought — right/wise, duty, or probability</h3><p>The source gives three distinct meanings.</p><h4>A. Right or wise</h4><p>If the intended meaning is that an action is right or wise, use:</p><ul><li>it is right for me to do it;</li><li>it is wise for me to do it.</li></ul><p>This is a judgment about whether the action is the right or wise thing to do.</p><h4>B. A person&#39;s business or duty</h4><p>If the intended meaning is that the action is the person&#39;s responsibility, use:</p><ul><li>it is my business to do it.</li></ul><p>This is not merely a suggestion that the action is wise. It expresses responsibility or duty.</p><h4>C. Probability from a fact</h4><p>The source gives this example:</p><ul><li>ordinary English: the train ought to be here in half an hour;</li><li>Basic explanation: it is probable from the fact that it will be so.</li></ul><p>Here ought does not express a duty for the train. It expresses an expectation based on the fact given.</p><p>Keep these three readings separate:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Intended force</th><th>Basic expression</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>right / wise</td><td>it is right / wise for me to do it</td></tr><tr><td>duty / responsibility</td><td>it is my business to do it</td></tr><tr><td>probability from evidence</td><td>it is probable from the fact that it will be so</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not automatically read every use of ought as an order or obligation.</p><h3>3. shall — statement or question</h3><p>The source makes a clear distinction between statements and questions.</p><h4>A. Shall in a statement</h4><p>In a statement that is not a question, the source says shall is covered by <strong>will</strong>.</p><p>Project-created illustration:</p><ul><li>ordinary English: I shall go;</li><li>Basic expression: I will go.</li></ul><p>This is the statement use. Do not apply it mechanically to questions.</p><h4>B. “Shall I do it?”</h4><p>The source gives two possible meanings:</p><ul><li><strong>Am I going to do it or not?</strong> — asking about intention or decision;</li><li><strong>Am I wise to do it?</strong> — asking whether the action is wise.</li></ul><p>The context determines which question is intended. Do not force one reading onto every use of “Shall I ...?”</p><h3>4. Fixed-group reinforcement: be able to</h3><p>The 50 Most Necessary Fixed Word-Groups include <strong>be able to</strong>.</p><p>Use it to reinforce the ability meaning of can:</p><ul><li>can do it (ability) → be able to do it.</li></ul><p>It does not replace the possibility meaning of can, the duty or probability meanings of ought, or the statement/question distinction of shall.</p><p>The fixed group supports the lesson; it is not a separate modal rule.</p><h3>5. Keep condition separate</h3><p>The source explains <strong>unless</strong> as <strong>if ... not</strong> on p.260. This is a conditional-clause relation, not one of the can / ought / shall meanings.</p><ul><li>unless I do it → if I do not do it.</li></ul><p>This belongs to the existing conditional/sentence-structure foundation. C09 may use it as a boundary check, but does not reteach conditional sentences.</p><h3>6. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet can, ought or shall, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>can:</strong> Is the sentence about a person&#39;s ability, or about what is possible for the person?</li><li><strong>ought:</strong> Is it right/wise, the person&#39;s business/duty, or probable from a fact?</li><li><strong>shall:</strong> Is it a statement or a question?</li><li><strong>Shall I ...?:</strong> Is the speaker asking about intention, or whether the action is wise?</li><li><strong>unless:</strong> Is this a conditional relation that belongs in sentence structure?</li></ol><p>Identify the force before choosing the Basic expression.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. can — ability or possibility</h4><p>Choose the expression that preserves the intended meaning.</p><ol><li>A person has the ability to do the action.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be able to do it</li><li>B. it is probable from the fact that it will be so</li><li>C. it is my business to do it</li></ul><ol><li>The intended meaning is that it is possible for the person to do the action.</li></ol><ul><li>A. be able to do it</li><li>B. it is possible for me to do it</li><li>C. it is right for me to do it</li></ul><ol><li>Someone says, “I can do it,” but gives no further context. What must the learner check?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Whether the sentence means ability or possibility</li><li>B. Whether the sentence means a duty</li><li>C. Whether it is a question with shall</li></ul><ol><li>What does the fixed group be able to directly reinforce?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Ability</li><li>B. Every meaning of can</li><li>C. Probability</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Be able to expresses the person&#39;s ability.</li><li>B — It is possible for me to do it expresses possibility.</li><li>A — The source gives both ability and possibility readings for can. Context selects the intended relation.</li><li>A — Be able to reinforces ability; it does not replace the possibility reading.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. ought — right/wise, duty, or probability</h4><p>Choose the meaning that matches the context.</p><ol><li>The intended meaning is that doing the action is right or wise.</li></ol><ul><li>A. it is right / wise for me to do it</li><li>B. it is probable from the fact that it will be so</li><li>C. I will do it</li></ul><ol><li>The intended meaning is that the action is the person&#39;s responsibility.</li></ol><ul><li>A. it is possible for me to do it</li><li>B. it is my business to do it</li><li>C. I am going to do it or not</li></ul><ol><li>The train ought to be here in half an hour. Which meaning does the source explain?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The train has a duty to arrive.</li><li>B. It is probable from the fact that it will be so.</li><li>C. It is wise for the train to arrive.</li></ul><ol><li>Does every use of ought express a duty?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes, it always expresses duty.</li><li>B. No. It may express right/wisdom, duty, or probability from a fact.</li></ul><ol><li>A statement is about what is expected because of a known fact. Which ought reading is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Probability from evidence</li><li>B. A person&#39;s duty</li><li>C. A question about intention</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — This is the source&#39;s right/wise meaning.</li><li>B — The source uses “it is my business to do it” for the duty/responsibility meaning.</li><li>B — The train example is an expectation based on the fact, not a duty imposed on the train.</li><li>B — The source gives three meanings; they must not be collapsed into duty.</li><li>A — The expectation is based on a fact, so this is the probability reading.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. shall — statement or question</h4><p>Choose the Basic expression that matches the sentence.</p><ol><li>Shall is used in a statement, not a question.</li></ol><ul><li>A. will</li><li>B. am I wise to do it?</li><li>C. it is my business to do it</li></ul><ol><li>“Shall I do it?” is asking whether the speaker is going to do it or not.</li></ol><ul><li>A. Am I going to do it or not?</li><li>B. Am I wise to do it?</li><li>C. It is probable from the fact that it will be so.</li></ul><ol><li>“Shall I do it?” is asking whether the action is wise.</li></ol><ul><li>A. Am I going to do it or not?</li><li>B. Am I wise to do it?</li><li>C. I am able to do it.</li></ul><ol><li>Without context, should every “Shall I ...?” question be assigned only one of these meanings?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes, it always asks about intention.</li><li>B. No. It may ask about intention or whether the action is wise.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — In non-question statements, the source says shall is covered by will.</li><li>A — This is the intention/decision reading.</li><li>B — This is the advisability reading.</li><li>B — The source gives both readings; the context determines which one is intended.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. Keep the boundary clear</h4><ol><li>A sentence uses unless. Which foundation handles it?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Conditional sentence structure. Unless is expressed with if and not; it is not one of the C09 modal meanings.</p><ol><li>A learner uses be able to. Which C09 meaning does this reinforce?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Ability. It does not automatically express possibility, duty or probability.</p><h4>E. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the force described by each Basic expression.</p><ol><li>be able to do it;</li><li>it is possible for me to do it;</li><li>it is right for me to do it;</li><li>it is wise for me to do it;</li><li>it is my business to do it;</li><li>it is probable from the fact that it will be so;</li><li>will in a statement;</li><li>Am I going to do it or not?</li><li>Am I wise to do it?</li><li>if I do not do it.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>The person has the ability to do it.</li><li>It is possible for the person to do it.</li><li>Doing it is the right thing.</li><li>Doing it is wise.</li><li>It is the person&#39;s duty or responsibility.</li><li>The fact supports the expectation that it will be so.</li><li>The statement use of shall.</li><li>A question about intention or decision.</li><li>A question about whether the action is wise.</li><li>The conditional expression used for unless.</li></ol></div></details><h4>F. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation:</p><ul><li>a person has the ability to do something;</li><li>an action is possible for a person;</li><li>an action is right or wise;</li><li>an action is someone&#39;s responsibility;</li><li>an event is probable from a known fact;</li><li>a statement uses shall;</li><li>a question with “Shall I ...?” asks about intention;</li><li>a question with “Shall I ...?” asks whether an action is wise.</li></ul><p>First identify the force. Then choose the Basic expression that preserves it.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C09 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary modal sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>distinguish ability from possibility in can;</li><li>distinguish right/wisdom, duty/responsibility and probability from a fact in ought;</li><li>use will for shall in statements;</li><li>distinguish the two readings of “Shall I ...?”;</li><li>use be able to as an ability expression without treating it as a replacement for every modal meaning;</li><li>keep unless with conditional sentence structure.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 10 — Quantity, Amount &amp; Degree.</p>","m2-l10":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> source-reviewed and internally reviewed teaching-content draft. It is based on <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, printed pp.257–258, and the accepted C10 teaching flow. It does not reteach basic number names or counting from the 45-Step foundation. The source distinctions among each, altogether, whole, few, many, too and once are kept separate; the past-time sense of once is cross-linked to C11. All 30 closed practice prompts have matching answers. Actual learner time and user feedback remain unmeasured; the lesson is not user-tested or App-integrated.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>The words <strong>each</strong>, <strong>altogether</strong>, <strong>whole</strong>, <strong>few</strong>, <strong>many</strong>, <strong>too</strong> and <strong>once</strong> are not entries in the 850-word list. This lesson teaches the meanings and Basic expressions the source gives for them.</p><p>The learner identifies the relation:</p><ol><li>distribution to members one by one;</li><li>the total count or the sense of completely;</li><li>a group taken as a whole or a story that is complete;</li><li>a small number or a great number;</li><li>excess amount, a degree that prevents an action, or addition;</li><li>one occurrence only or a past-time meaning.</li></ol><p>Number names such as one and six belong to the existing number foundation. C10 teaches the quantity/degree relation, not number names.</p><h3>1. each — distribution to every member</h3><p>The source gives this situation:</p><ul><li>each person in a group gets something;</li><li>the distribution is made to every one (separately).</li></ul><p>The important relation is <strong>separate distribution</strong>: the members are considered one by one.</p><p>Do not confuse this with the total count of the group. That is the quantity sense illustrated by altogether.</p><h3>2. altogether — total count or completely</h3><p>The source gives two different uses.</p><h4>A. Total count</h4><p>The source uses the example of six people “altogether”. The point is the total number in the group, not a separate distribution to each person.</p><p>It then explains the result with the words “there would make six”. Treat this as the source&#39;s illustration of the total-count meaning; do not turn it into a universal word-for-word formula for every sentence containing altogether.</p><h4>B. Completely / quite</h4><p>In a statement such as <strong>altogether wrong</strong>, altogether means <strong>quite</strong> or <strong>completely</strong> so.</p><p>This is a degree or completeness meaning, not a count.</p><p>Keep the two readings separate:</p><ul><li><strong>six of them altogether</strong> — the total count is six;</li><li><strong>altogether wrong</strong> — quite or completely wrong.</li></ul><h3>3. whole — all of a group or a complete thing</h3><p>The source gives two examples:</p><ul><li><strong>the whole group</strong> → all the group;</li><li><strong>the whole story</strong> → the complete story.</li></ul><p>The word whole can therefore point to all members of a group or to something that is complete. Use the noun and context to identify which relation is intended.</p><p>Do not treat whole as a synonym for each: each distributes to members separately; whole views the group as one complete unit.</p><h3>4. few and many — small or great number</h3><p>The source gives:</p><ul><li><strong>few</strong> → a small number;</li><li><strong>many</strong> → a great number or quite a number.</li></ul><p>The source says that “few men” are very frequently two or three. It does not define few as exactly two or three. When the exact number is uncertain, it is safer to say <strong>a small number</strong>.</p><p>The contrast is about the size of the number:</p><ul><li><strong>few</strong> — a small number;</li><li><strong>many</strong> — a great number / quite a number.</li></ul><p>Do not use few and many as if they described the same amount.</p><h3>5. too — excess, disabling degree or addition</h3><p>The source gives three different uses. They must not be collapsed into one translation.</p><h4>A. Excess amount</h4><ul><li><strong>too many</strong> → more than enough (the source also gives overmuch as a gloss).</li></ul><p>This is about the amount being greater than what is needed.</p><h4>B. A degree that prevents an action</h4><ul><li><strong>too tired to go</strong> → so tired that the person is unable to go.</li></ul><p>Here too does not merely mean “very”. The degree of tiredness is so great that the person cannot go.</p><h4>C. Addition</h4><ul><li><strong>ill too</strong> → ill in addition.</li></ul><p>Here too means that the person is ill as well; it adds another condition.</p><p>Compare the three:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>too many</td><td>More than enough in amount</td></tr><tr><td>too tired to go</td><td>The degree prevents the action</td></tr><tr><td>ill too</td><td>Another condition is added</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>6. once — one time or a past time</h3><p>The source gives two different meanings.</p><h4>A. One occurrence only</h4><ul><li><strong>once</strong> → one time only.</li></ul><p>This is about the number of times something happens. It belongs in C10.</p><h4>B. True at an earlier time</h4><p>When something was <strong>once</strong> true, the source explains it as true <strong>at one time</strong> or <strong>in the past</strong>.</p><p>This is a time meaning, not a count of one occurrence. Its primary teaching home is C11 Time &amp; Sequence; C10 links to it so the learner does not confuse the two meanings.</p><h3>7. How to choose the expression</h3><p>When you meet one of these words, ask:</p><ol><li><strong>each:</strong> Are members receiving or doing something separately?</li><li><strong>altogether:</strong> Is the sentence giving a total count, or does it mean completely/quite?</li><li><strong>whole:</strong> Is it all the members of a group, or a complete thing?</li><li><strong>few / many:</strong> Is the number small or great?</li><li><strong>too:</strong> Does it mean more than enough, a degree that prevents an action, or in addition?</li><li><strong>once:</strong> Does it mean one time only, or at one time/in the past?</li></ol><p>Do not choose a translation from the word alone. Identify the relation in the sentence.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Distribution, total count and whole</h4><p>Choose the meaning that matches the source example.</p><ol><li>Each person in a group gets something. What is the important relation?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The distribution is made to every one (separately).</li><li>B. The whole group is one complete unit.</li><li>C. The number is more than enough.</li></ul><ol><li>There are six people altogether. What does altogether express here?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The total count is six.</li><li>B. Each person is considered separately.</li><li>C. The group is completely wrong.</li></ul><ol><li>A statement says that a number is altogether wrong. Which meaning is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The number is counted one by one.</li><li>B. The number is quite or completely wrong.</li><li>C. The number is a small number.</li></ul><ol><li>The whole group is present. Which Basic expression does the source give?</li></ol><ul><li>A. all the group</li><li>B. every one separately</li><li>C. more than enough</li></ul><ol><li>The whole story is known. Which Basic expression does the source give?</li></ol><ul><li>A. a great number</li><li>B. the complete story</li><li>C. one time only</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Each concerns distribution to every member separately.</li><li>A — Altogether gives the total count in this example.</li><li>B — In altogether wrong, altogether means quite or completely.</li><li>A — The source explains the whole group as all the group.</li><li>B — The source explains the whole story as the complete story.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. few and many</h4><p>Choose the expression that preserves the amount.</p><ol><li>The intended meaning is a small number of people.</li></ol><ul><li>A. a small number</li><li>B. a great number</li><li>C. more than enough</li></ul><ol><li>The intended meaning is a great number of people.</li></ol><ul><li>A. a small number</li><li>B. a great number / quite a number</li><li>C. every one separately</li></ul><ol><li>The source says few men are frequently two or three. Does this make few an exact number meaning two or three?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes, always exactly two or three.</li><li>B. No. The source says this is frequent, but recommends a small number when uncertain.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Few is expressed as a small number.</li><li>B — Many is expressed as a great number or quite a number.</li><li>B — The source does not make few an exact number. It recommends the clearer expression a small number when the count is uncertain.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. too — three different meanings</h4><p>Choose the meaning that matches the context.</p><ol><li>There are too many people for the room.</li></ol><ul><li>A. More than enough in amount</li><li>B. So tired that a person cannot go</li><li>C. Ill in addition</li></ul><ol><li>A person is too tired to go.</li></ol><ul><li>A. The person is ill as well.</li><li>B. The person is so tired that they are unable to go.</li><li>C. There are more than enough people.</li></ul><ol><li>A person is ill too.</li></ol><ul><li>A. The person is ill in addition.</li><li>B. The person is too tired to go.</li><li>C. The number is completely wrong.</li></ul><ol><li>Do all three examples use too with the same meaning?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes. It always means very.</li><li>B. No. It can express excess amount, a degree that prevents an action, or addition.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — Too many expresses an amount greater than enough.</li><li>B — Too tired to go expresses a degree that prevents the action.</li><li>A — Ill too means ill in addition.</li><li>B — The source gives three different uses; too is not a single translation.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. once — count or past time</h4><p>Choose the meaning that matches the sentence.</p><ol><li>A thing is done once, one time only.</li></ol><ul><li>A. It is done one time only.</li><li>B. It was true in the past.</li><li>C. The whole thing is complete.</li></ul><ol><li>Something that was once true was true at one time or in the past.</li></ol><ul><li>A. It happened one time only.</li><li>B. It was true at one time / in the past.</li><li>C. It happened to every member separately.</li></ul><ol><li>Which lesson is the primary home for the past-time sense of once?</li></ol><ul><li>A. C10 — Quantity, Amount &amp; Degree</li><li>B. C11 — Time &amp; Sequence</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — This is the one-occurrence meaning and belongs in C10.</li><li>B — This is the past-time meaning.</li><li>B — The past-time meaning belongs in C11; C10 keeps a cross-link to prevent confusion.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. Keep the foundation and neighbouring lessons separate</h4><ol><li>A learner needs to say the number six. Which part of the course provides number names?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> The existing number foundation in the 45 Steps. C10 does not reteach number names.</p><ol><li>Someone says “the house next to this one”. Is next being used as a time word?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. It describes spatial adjacency and cross-links to C04 Movement &amp; Position.</p><ol><li>A person says that something was once true in the past. Which lesson is the primary home for this meaning?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> C11 Time &amp; Sequence. C10 teaches the separate meaning once = one time only.</p><h4>F. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the relation described by each Basic expression.</p><ol><li>the distribution is made to every one (separately);</li><li>six of them altogether;</li><li>quite or completely wrong;</li><li>all the group;</li><li>the complete story;</li><li>a small number;</li><li>a great number / quite a number;</li><li>more than enough;</li><li>so tired that the person is unable to go;</li><li>ill in addition;</li><li>one time only;</li><li>at one time / in the past.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Each member receives something separately.</li><li>The total count is six.</li><li>The thing is completely wrong.</li><li>All members of the group.</li><li>The story is complete.</li><li>Few; the number is small.</li><li>Many; the number is great.</li><li>Too many; the amount is greater than enough.</li><li>The person&#39;s tiredness is so great that the person cannot go.</li><li>The person is ill as well; another condition is added.</li><li>Once, in the one-occurrence sense.</li><li>Once, in the past-time sense; this belongs primarily to C11.</li></ol></div></details><h4>G. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose one situation:</p><ul><li>distributing something to every member separately;</li><li>giving the total number in a group;</li><li>saying that something is completely wrong;</li><li>describing all members of a group or a complete story;</li><li>describing a small or great number;</li><li>expressing an amount greater than enough;</li><li>describing a degree that prevents an action;</li><li>adding another condition;</li><li>expressing one occurrence only;</li><li>describing something that was true in the past.</li></ul><p>First identify the relation. Then choose the source expression that preserves it.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this open response is not automatically scored by the current App. It requires a separately authored and tested set of C10 patterns before the App may claim to check arbitrary quantity/degree sentences.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>distinguish separate distribution from a total count;</li><li>distinguish altogether as total count from altogether as completely/quite;</li><li>express a whole group and a complete story;</li><li>distinguish a small number from a great number;</li><li>distinguish the three uses of too;</li><li>distinguish once = one time only from once = at one time/in the past;</li><li>keep the past-time sense of once in C11;</li><li>use the existing number foundation rather than repeating number names in C10.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 Lesson 11 — Time &amp; Sequence.</p>","m2-l11":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> first complete teaching-content draft, based on <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, printed pp.258–260, and the accepted C11 teaching flow. The source&#39;s different contexts are kept separate. Closed exercises have answer keys. Actual learner time and feedback are unmeasured; this draft is not user-tested or App-integrated.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>This is not a lesson on reading clocks, naming dates, counting time or learning basic tense forms. Those belong to the existing foundations.</p><p>This lesson teaches the learner to identify the time relation in a sentence:</p><ul><li>a situation was true during an earlier period;</li><li>an event happened before the present point;</li><li>an account covers events through the present point;</li><li>an expected event has not happened by now;</li><li>an event is related to an earlier starting point;</li><li>an action follows another, or the same action happens on the next occasion;</li><li>a state or relationship continues through time.</li></ul><p>The same ordinary-English word can express different relations. The whole sentence decides which meaning is intended.</p><h3>1. once and ago — an earlier period</h3><h4>once</h4><p>The source gives two meanings:</p><ul><li>a single occurrence — Module 2 · Lesson 10;</li><li>something that was true during an earlier period — this lesson.</li></ul><p>Here, once places a situation in an earlier period. It does not count how many times an event happened.</p><h4>ago</h4><p>For its long-period example, the source gives the Basic expression <strong>a long time back</strong>. It also gives another phrase referring to earlier days.</p><p>Keep the scope of the example. The source passage does not establish a universal formula for every numbered duration followed by ago.</p><h3>2. already, the present-reaching account and the two uses of yet</h3><p>These uses relate an event or state to the present point, but they are not interchangeable.</p><h4>already</h4><p>The source gives two contexts:</p><ul><li>An event that has already taken place happened before the present point.</li><li>When a person is already at the expected place, the person is there now.</li></ul><p>The first is about when an event happened; the second is about a person&#39;s present location. Do not use the event meaning to explain the location example.</p><h4>The present-reaching account</h4><p>An account of events may cover what has happened from an earlier period through the present point. The source expresses this with <strong>so far</strong> and <strong>up to now</strong>.</p><p>This describes the time covered by the account. It is not simply another way to say that one event happened already.</p><h4>yet in a negative sentence</h4><p>If an expected person or event is not present by the current point, the meaning is that the expected arrival or event has still not happened.</p><p>The relation is <strong>not completed by now</strong>.</p><h4>The joining use of yet</h4><p>The source calls this joining form <strong>and yet</strong> and explains it as meaning <strong>at the same time</strong>.</p><p>Keep the two uses apart:</p><ul><li>negative yet — an expected event has not happened by now;</li><li>the joining use — two statements hold together.</li></ul><h3>3. since — a time relation or a reason</h3><p>The source gives three contexts.</p><h4>A. since with a past-year reference</h4><p>The source&#39;s example concerns friends who have not been seen from a past-year starting point. It explains the whole idea as not having seen them over an extended period.</p><p>That is a paraphrase of the complete sentence. It does not make since a universal substitute for that duration.</p><h4>B. since then</h4><p>When the source describes events that have taken place since a particular earlier point, it explains that those events took place <strong>after then</strong>.</p><p>The earlier point is the reference; the sentence identifies events that happened later than it.</p><h4>C. since giving a reason</h4><p>In the source&#39;s example about lateness and going to bed, since gives a reason. Its Basic expression is <strong>because</strong>.</p><p>This is not a time meaning. It belongs to the sentence-relation foundation and is included here to prevent confusion with the time uses.</p><h3>4. next — sequence, repetition or position</h3><p>The source gives three different uses.</p><h4>A. The following action</h4><p>When a sentence asks what will be done next, it refers to the action that follows the present one in the sequence.</p><h4>B. The next occasion of the same action</h4><p>When a sentence says that a person next does the same thing, it means that the person repeats that action on the next occasion. It does not mean that the person chooses a different action in a list.</p><h4>C. Spatial position</h4><p>When next is used to say that one house is beside another, the source describes the first house as the closest to the reference house.</p><p>This is spatial adjacency, not time or sequence. It cross-links to Module 2 · Lesson 4 — Movement &amp; Position.</p><h3>5. always — general continuation, frequency or future continuation</h3><p>The source gives several contexts:</p><ul><li>The general meaning is <strong>at all times</strong>.</li><li>In some connections, the intended meaning may be that something happens often.</li><li>When the future is specially in view, as in a statement about remaining someone&#39;s friend, the source says <strong>for ever</strong> is a better way of putting it.</li><li>The source also notes that descriptions meaning unchanged or continuing without an end are right in other connections.</li></ul><p>These are context-dependent choices. Do not teach always as one fixed frequency word, and do not use the future expression as a universal replacement.</p><h3>6. How to choose</h3><p>Ask these questions before choosing an expression:</p><ol><li>Is the sentence placing a situation in an earlier period, or describing what is true now?</li><li>Is an event completed before now, or is a person simply present at a place?</li><li>Does the account cover events through the present point?</li><li>Is the expected event still outstanding, or is yet joining two statements?</li><li>Does since refer to a past point, a later event, or a reason?</li><li>Does next identify the following action, a repeated action, or a spatial relation?</li><li>Does always mean continuous truth, frequent occurrence in this context, or future continuation?</li></ol><p>Do not choose a paraphrase from the target word alone. Identify the relation in the whole sentence.</p><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. Earlier time or present location</h4><p>Choose the meaning that matches the context.</p><ol><li>Something was once true. What time relation is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. It was true during an earlier period.</li><li>B. It must have happened only one time.</li><li>C. It is true at every moment.</li></ul><ol><li>A thing was done once, and the sentence counts its occurrences. Which lesson handles this meaning?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Module 2 · Lesson 10 — Quantity, Amount &amp; Degree.</li><li>B. Module 2 · Lesson 11 — Time &amp; Sequence.</li></ul><ol><li>The source&#39;s long-period ago example uses which Basic paraphrase?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The expression recorded in Section 1.</li><li>B. The expression for a later event.</li><li>C. The general always meaning.</li></ul><ol><li>An event has already taken place. What is the intended relation?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The event happened before the present point.</li><li>B. The event has not happened by now.</li><li>C. The event will happen on the next occasion.</li></ul><ol><li>A person is already present at the expected place. What does the sentence tell us?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The person is currently at that place.</li><li>B. The person will go there next.</li><li>C. The person is absent.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — This is the past-time sense of once; it does not count occurrences.</li><li>A — C10 handles one occurrence; C11 handles a situation that was true during an earlier period.</li><li>A — This is the source&#39;s Basic expression for its long-period example.</li><li>A — Already describes the event as having taken place before the present point.</li><li>A — This is the source&#39;s separate present-location context.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. Events through now, negative yet and the joining use</h4><ol><li>An account includes events from an earlier period through the present point. Which description matches the source?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The source&#39;s expression for an account covering events through now.</li><li>B. An account only of what will happen later.</li><li>C. An account of one event only.</li></ul><ol><li>A person is expected at a place but has not arrived by the present point. Which relation is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The person is still absent.</li><li>B. The person is already present.</li><li>C. The person will arrive on the next occasion.</li></ul><ol><li>The source&#39;s negative-yet example says that the person has not come by now. What does this test?</li></ol><ul><li>A. A past event.</li><li>B. An event not completed by the present point.</li><li>C. A repeated action.</li></ul><ol><li>The joining use of yet has which meaning?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Two statements hold together.</li><li>B. An event has not happened by now.</li><li>C. An action follows another.</li></ul><ol><li>Are negative yet and the joining use of yet the same?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes. Both always mean that an event has not happened.</li><li>B. No. One concerns an outstanding event; the other joins statements that hold together.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source uses this description for an account covering events through the present point.</li><li>A — Negative yet describes the expected arrival as not having happened by now.</li><li>B — The event remains outstanding at the present reference point.</li><li>A — The joining use means that both statements hold together.</li><li>B — The two contexts must not be collapsed into one translation.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. since — time or reason</h4><ol><li>The source&#39;s example concerns friends not seen from a past-year starting point and paraphrases the whole idea as not having seen them over an extended period. What must the learner remember?</li></ol><ul><li>A. This explains the whole example; it does not make since a universal substitute for that duration.</li><li>B. Since always means an extended period.</li><li>C. Since always gives a reason.</li></ul><ol><li>Events that have taken place since a particular earlier point are described as taking place:</li></ol><ul><li>A. before that point.</li><li>B. later than that point.</li><li>C. at the same moment as that point.</li></ul><ol><li>In the source&#39;s example about it being late and going to bed, since gives:</li></ol><ul><li>A. the reason for the action.</li><li>B. a repeated action.</li><li>C. a nearby position.</li></ul><ol><li>Which context uses since as a reason rather than a time relation?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Events after a particular earlier point.</li><li>B. Friends not seen from a past-year starting point.</li><li>C. The statement that it is late is used to explain the decision to go to bed.</li></ul><ol><li>Should every use of since be interpreted as a time starting point?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes.</li><li>B. No. The source also uses it to give a reason.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source paraphrases the complete friend example. It does not establish one fixed replacement for every since sentence.</li><li>B — The earlier point is the reference; the events happened later.</li><li>A — In this context since gives the reason for the action.</li><li>C — The statement about lateness gives the reason for the action.</li><li>B — The learner must distinguish time from reason.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. next — sequence, repetition or position</h4><ol><li>The sentence asks what will be done next. Which relation is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The action that follows in the sequence.</li><li>B. The same action happened earlier.</li><li>C. A place is beside another place.</li></ul><ol><li>The sentence says that a person next performs the same action. What does next indicate?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The next occasion on which the same action is repeated.</li><li>B. A different action that must happen first.</li><li>C. A place beside the person.</li></ul><ol><li>One house is described as immediately beside another. Which relation is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Time sequence.</li><li>B. Spatial closeness.</li><li>C. Repetition.</li></ul><ol><li>Which use of next belongs to Module 2 · Lesson 4 rather than this lesson&#39;s time/sequence teaching?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The action that follows.</li><li>B. The next occasion of the same action.</li><li>C. The spatial relation between two houses.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The source describes the action that follows the present one.</li><li>A — Here next marks the next occurrence of the same action.</li><li>B — This is a spatial relation.</li><li>C — Spatial adjacency cross-links to Movement &amp; Position.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. always — general meaning or a particular context</h4><ol><li>What is the general meaning of always given by the source?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Something remains true throughout the relevant times.</li><li>B. Only once.</li><li>C. Only after a particular event.</li></ul><ol><li>In some connections, what other sense may be intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Often.</li><li>B. Never.</li><li>C. Only before now.</li></ul><ol><li>When a future relationship is specially in view, what kind of expression does the source recommend for the example about remaining someone&#39;s friend?</li></ol><ul><li>A. One that describes continuation without an end.</li><li>B. One that refers to a past starting point.</li><li>C. One that refers to a long period before now.</li></ul><ol><li>Does the source say that the future-focused expression and the descriptions of being unchanged or endless are universal replacements for always?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes.</li><li>B. No. The source attaches them to particular contexts.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — This is the general meaning.</li><li>A — The source says that an often/frequent sense may fit some connections.</li><li>A — For a future relationship, the source recommends the expression given in Section 5.</li><li>B — These expressions are context-dependent, not interchangeable in every sentence.</li></ol></div></details><h4>F. Keep neighbouring lessons and foundations separate</h4><ol><li>A sentence means that an action happened one time only. Which lesson is its primary home?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Module 2 · Lesson 10 — Quantity, Amount &amp; Degree.</p><ol><li>A sentence means that something was true during an earlier period. Which lesson is its primary home?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Module 2 · Lesson 11 — Time &amp; Sequence.</p><ol><li>The word next describes a place beside another place. Which lesson provides the spatial foundation?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> Module 2 · Lesson 4 — Movement &amp; Position.</p><ol><li>Since gives a reason rather than a time relation. Where does that sentence relation belong?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> The existing sentence-relation foundation. C11 identifies the contrast but does not create a separate reason lesson.</p><ol><li>A learner needs to name a date, read a clock or count a period of time. Is that the main task of C11?</li></ol><p><strong>Answer:</strong> No. Those belong to the existing number/time foundation. C11 focuses on the additional meanings and context distinctions in the source.</p><h4>G. Reverse understanding</h4><p>Explain the relation described by each expression.</p><ol><li>A situation was true during an earlier period.</li><li>The source&#39;s Basic paraphrase for its long-period ago example.</li><li>An event happened before the present point.</li><li>An account includes events through the present point.</li><li>An expected person has not arrived by now.</li><li>Two statements hold together.</li><li>Events happened later than a particular earlier point.</li><li>The sentence gives a reason.</li><li>The action that follows in a sequence.</li><li>The next occurrence of the same action.</li><li>A place is spatially closest to another place.</li><li>Something is true continuously.</li><li>Something happens often in the particular context.</li><li>A future relationship is intended to continue without an end.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>Once in its past-time sense.</li><li>The source&#39;s Basic paraphrase for its long-period example.</li><li>Already in the completed-event context.</li><li>The source&#39;s present-reaching account of events.</li><li>Negative yet.</li><li>The joining use of yet.</li><li>The time use of since with a past starting point.</li><li>Since in its reason use.</li><li>Next in sequence.</li><li>Next referring to the next occasion of the same action.</li><li>Next in its spatial use; cross-link to C04.</li><li>Always in its general sense.</li><li>Always in a context where often is intended.</li><li>Always in a future-focused context; use the source&#39;s future-continuation expression.</li></ol></div></details><h4>H. Independent construction</h4><p>Choose two situations and explain the time relation before constructing a Basic expression:</p><ul><li>something was true during an earlier period;</li><li>an event happened before the present point;</li><li>an account covers events through now;</li><li>an expected event has not happened by now;</li><li>two statements hold together;</li><li>an event happened after a stated earlier point;</li><li>a sentence gives a reason;</li><li>an action follows another action;</li><li>the same action happens on the next occasion;</li><li>something continues through time;</li><li>something happens often in a particular context;</li><li>a future relationship is intended to continue without an end.</li></ul><p>First identify the relation. Then choose the expression that preserves it. Finally, explain why a neighbouring meaning does not fit.</p><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> this independent response is not automatically scored by the current App. The existing checker must not be described as evaluating arbitrary C11 sentences until a separate C11 rule set has been designed and tested.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>distinguish the past-time sense of once from the one-occurrence sense in C10;</li><li>explain the source&#39;s long-period ago example without inventing a universal ago formula;</li><li>distinguish already for an earlier event from already describing a person&#39;s present location;</li><li>describe an account of events through the present point;</li><li>distinguish negative yet from the joining use of yet;</li><li>distinguish since as a time relation from since giving a reason;</li><li>distinguish next as sequence, repetition or spatial position;</li><li>distinguish always as general continuation, context-dependent frequency or future continuation;</li><li>keep the basic clock, calendar, number and tense foundations separate from this lesson.</li></ul><p><strong>Next lesson:</strong> Module 2 · Lesson 12 — Context-Dependent Words &amp; Paraphrase.</p>","m2-l12":"<blockquote><p><strong>Draft status:</strong> first complete teaching-content draft, based on <em>The ABC of Basic English</em>, printed pp.260–262, and the source-checked C12 teaching flow. Internal review: 22 multiple-choice prompts with 22 matching answer keys; 15 reverse-understanding prompts with 15 suggested answers; 4 independent-construction tasks. Actual learner time and feedback are unmeasured; this draft is not user-tested or App-integrated.</p></blockquote><h3>What this lesson teaches</h3><p>Some ordinary-English words are not entries in the Basic 850. The learner must still be able to express their meanings.</p><p>The task is not to find one replacement word and memorize it. It is to:</p><ol><li>identify what the ordinary-English word refers to in this sentence;</li><li>choose the Basic description that preserves that meaning;</li><li>check that the description still refers to the same person, thing, place or condition.</li></ol><p>This lesson uses seven examples from the source: busy, deaf, world, life, hill, home and address. Each needs context. The lesson does not claim that these seven examples provide a complete method for every word outside the Basic list.</p><h3>1. busy and deaf — describe the condition</h3><h4>busy</h4><p>The source first gives a description built around the Name business. It then says that a more natural description is preferable.</p><p>Choose according to the intended meaning:</p><ul><li>If the sentence emphasizes that the person has many tasks waiting, the Basic description is <strong>has much to do</strong>.</li><li>If it emphasizes the person&#39;s active effort, use <strong>working hard</strong>. This describes what the person is doing, not a job or profession.</li></ul><p>The source&#39;s first description is not the preferred everyday form. Do not teach busy as meaning that someone is a business person. Here business refers to work or tasks, not the commercial exchange/sale topic in Module 2 · Lesson 7.</p><h4>deaf</h4><p>Describe the person&#39;s hearing condition. A simple learner-built paraphrase is <strong>poor hearing</strong>.</p><p>This does not say that the person cannot speak, cannot understand, or has any other condition. Add only what the sentence supports.</p><h4>What to notice</h4><p>Busy and deaf are ordinary-English Quality words outside the Basic list. The task is to describe the condition with Basic words. This is not the Module 1 lesson on extending the meaning of an already-known Basic Quality.</p><h3>2. world — identify what the word refers to</h3><p>The source gives three different readings.</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>Meaning to identify</th><th>Basic description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A statement about the physical world or space</td><td>The planet itself</td><td>earth</td></tr><tr><td>A statement about the people of the world as one group</td><td>All the people being considered</td><td>every one</td></tr><tr><td>A statement about the state of society or what is changing</td><td>The state of society</td><td>things or conditions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The same ordinary-English word does not have one fixed Basic replacement. The sentence decides which of the three readings in the table is intended.</p><h3>3. life — an abstract sense, living objects or the activity of living</h3><p>The source distinguishes three uses:</p><ol><li><strong>Abstract meaning:</strong> life can refer to the fact that something exists. The Basic meaning is <strong>existence</strong>.</li><li><strong>Objects that have life:</strong> when the sentence refers to people, animals or plants as living objects, use <strong>living things</strong> or all of these objects.</li><li><strong>The activity itself:</strong> the source also shows the -ing form used by itself as a Name for the activity of living.</li></ol><p>Keep these apart. A sentence about the abstract sense of life is not necessarily talking about the living objects themselves. A sentence about the activity is different again.</p><p>The third use cross-links to the existing -ing word-form foundation. It does not make the -ing form a universal replacement for every use of life. Module 2 · Lesson 8 covers the event of dying and the source&#39;s expressions for it; C12 covers the noun life&#39;s contextual meanings, not the death event.</p><h3>4. hill — one raised landform or a region</h3><p>A hill is a piece of land that stands higher than the land around it.</p><p>The source gives <strong>a slope</strong> as one short description. It also allows a comparison with a mountain-like landform when the size fits the particular case.</p><p>Do not force the mountain comparison onto every hill. The scale and shape matter.</p><p>The source also discusses a whole region whose land contains many hills. That is not one hill. Describe the region itself, for example as an area or country with many hills.</p><p><strong>850-word boundary:</strong> the source mentions highlands for this regional context, but that term is absent from the project&#39;s 850-word list. Do not present it as a Basic word. Use a description built from Basic words instead.</p><h3>5. home — residence or family state</h3><h4>The place where a person lives</h4><p>Home refers to the place where the person actually lives. It does not require the person to own a house or to live in a separate building.</p><p>A short stay in a hotel does not, by itself, make that hotel the person&#39;s home.</p><h4>The family-state context</h4><p>The source also gives a context in which the intended meaning is <strong>with his family</strong>.</p><p>This is a particular reading about the person&#39;s family situation. It is not a universal replacement for home. When the sentence is about the place of residence, keep the place meaning.</p><h3>6. address — identify which address is meant</h3><p>The source gives three contexts:</p><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Context</th><th>What the ordinary-English word means</th><th>Basic description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Private address</td><td>Where the person lives</td><td>The place where the person lives</td></tr><tr><td>Business address</td><td>The location connected with the person&#39;s business</td><td>place of business</td></tr><tr><td>Address written at the top of a letter</td><td>The heading of the letter</td><td>letter-heading</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not assume that address always means a person&#39;s home. A private address, a business address and the heading written on a letter are different things.</p><p><strong>Scope:</strong> this lesson covers address as a Name for a location or a letter heading. It does not add the verb use of address someone.</p><h3>7. The context-first paraphrase method</h3><p>Use the same short method for every target word:</p><ol><li><strong>Find the target.</strong> Which ordinary-English word needs a Basic description?</li><li><strong>Read the whole context.</strong> What person, thing, place, condition or activity is being referred to?</li><li><strong>Choose the sense.</strong> Which of the source&#39;s meanings fits this sentence?</li><li><strong>Build the description.</strong> Use Basic words and the word forms already taught.</li><li><strong>Read it back.</strong> Does the Basic description preserve the same meaning?</li><li><strong>Check uncertainty.</strong> If two meanings still fit, do not guess. Ask for more context or keep the broader description.</li></ol><p>The method is the same, but the actual Basic expression changes with the meaning.</p><h3>8. Keep this lesson separate from the other curriculum topics</h3><div class=\"expr-table-wrap\"><table class=\"expr-table\"><thead><tr><th>Curriculum topic</th><th>Starting point</th><th>Learner&#39;s task</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Module 2 · Lesson 12 (this lesson)</td><td>An ordinary-English word outside the Basic list</td><td>Identify its contextual meaning and express it with Basic words</td></tr><tr><td>Module 1 · Lesson 3</td><td>A word already in the Basic list</td><td>Use context to identify a narrower referent within that word&#39;s meaning</td></tr><tr><td>Module 1 · Lessons 1–2</td><td>A word already in the Basic list</td><td>Understand source-recognized related meaning expansion or use as a Thing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The direction of the task matters. C12 is not a second meaning-expansion lesson and is not a second Special Use / narrowing lesson.</p><p>Other boundaries:</p><ul><li><strong>unless → if ... not</strong> remains in the conditional-sentence foundation. It is not added to this Lesson.</li><li><strong>finish</strong> is not a Basic replacement and is not added to this Lesson.</li><li>busy and deaf are not taught as Names of Qualities expansion.</li><li>The source&#39;s seven examples are the scope of this Lesson&#39;s source-based teaching. Do not imply that the current App can automatically paraphrase every word outside the 850.</li></ul><hr><h3>Practice</h3><h4>A. busy and deaf</h4><p>Choose the description that preserves the intended meaning.</p><ol><li>A sentence says that a person is busy because many tasks still need attention. Which kind of description fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. A description of the amount of work the person has to do.</li><li>B. A description of the person&#39;s business as a profession.</li><li>C. A description of the person&#39;s hearing.</li></ul><ol><li>A sentence emphasizes that a person is actively putting effort into work. Which kind of description fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. A description of the person&#39;s hearing.</li><li>B. A description of the work the person is doing.</li><li>C. A description of the person&#39;s home.</li></ul><ol><li>A person is described as deaf. Which condition must the paraphrase describe?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Hearing.</li><li>B. Speaking.</li><li>C. Movement.</li></ul><ol><li>Are busy and deaf entries in the project&#39;s Basic 850 list?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes. They are two Basic Qualities.</li><li>B. No. They are ordinary-English target words that this lesson describes with Basic words.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The context emphasizes the amount of work waiting to be done.</li><li>B — The context emphasizes the person&#39;s activity, not the number of tasks.</li><li>A — The source&#39;s description is about hearing. It does not state that the person cannot speak.</li><li>B — Both target words are absent from the current 850 list.</li></ol></div></details><h4>B. world and life</h4><ol><li>A sentence uses world to refer to the physical planet. Which reading is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The people considered as one group.</li><li>B. The physical planet.</li><li>C. The changing state of society.</li></ul><ol><li>A sentence uses world to refer to all the people considered together. Which reading is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The physical planet.</li><li>B. A private address.</li><li>C. The collective-people reading.</li></ul><ol><li>A sentence reports change in the world, and the context concerns the state of society. Which description fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The changing state of society.</li><li>B. A person&#39;s home.</li><li>C. A single raised landform.</li></ul><ol><li>A sentence uses life to mean the fact that something exists. Which sense is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The abstract sense.</li><li>B. The group of living objects.</li><li>C. The activity of learning.</li></ul><ol><li>A sentence uses life to refer to people, animals and plants as objects that have life. Which sense is intended?</li></ol><ul><li>A. A private location.</li><li>B. Living objects.</li><li>C. The physical planet.</li></ul><ol><li>The source uses an -ing form by itself as a Name for an activity. Which foundation does this example connect to?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The word-form foundation for -ing.</li><li>B. The number foundation.</li><li>C. The conditional-sentence foundation.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>B — The context is physical space, not people or social conditions.</li><li>C — The word refers to the people as a group.</li><li>A — The sentence concerns changing circumstances, not the planet itself.</li><li>A — The sentence is about the abstract meaning of life.</li><li>B — The reference is to living entities, not an abstract idea.</li><li>A — The source&#39;s example uses the -ing form as a Name. It cross-links to the existing word-form foundation.</li></ol></div></details><h4>C. hill, home and address</h4><ol><li>A sentence describes one raised piece of land. Which short description from the source fits?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The slope description.</li><li>B. The business-location description.</li><li>C. The family-state description.</li></ul><ol><li>Can every hill be described as a mountain?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes. Size never matters.</li><li>B. No. The comparison is used only when the size fits.</li><li>C. No. A hill always means a whole region.</li></ul><ol><li>A sentence refers to a whole region containing many hills, not one raised piece of land. What should the learner describe?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The region itself.</li><li>B. One person&#39;s private address.</li><li>C. One person&#39;s hearing.</li></ul><ol><li>Does home always mean a house owned by the person?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes.</li><li>B. No. It refers to where the person lives and does not require ownership of a house.</li></ul><ol><li>A person stays in a hotel for one night. Does that fact alone make the hotel the person&#39;s home?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes.</li><li>B. No. A short stay alone does not establish that it is the person&#39;s home.</li></ul><ol><li>In the source&#39;s family-state context, what is being described?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The person&#39;s family situation.</li><li>B. The address written on a letter.</li><li>C. A region of hills.</li></ul><ol><li>A form asks for a person&#39;s private address. Which information is required?</li></ol><ul><li>A. The place where the person lives.</li><li>B. The location of the person&#39;s business.</li><li>C. The heading at the top of a letter.</li></ul><ol><li>A business letter has an address for the business and another heading written at the top of the letter. Are these the same thing?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes. Both always mean the person&#39;s home.</li><li>B. No. One identifies the business location; the other is the letter&#39;s heading.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — The question is about one raised landform.</li><li>B — The source makes the comparison depend on size.</li><li>A — The referent is the region, not one hill.</li><li>B — A person&#39;s home is where the person lives; ownership is not the test.</li><li>B — A temporary hotel stay does not by itself establish a home.</li><li>A — This is the family-state context, not the place/address meaning.</li><li>A — A private address identifies where the person lives.</li><li>B — The business location and the heading written on a letter serve different purposes.</li></ol></div></details><h4>D. Keep C12 and neighbouring curriculum topics separate</h4><ol><li>A learner starts with an ordinary-English word outside the Basic list and must describe its meaning with Basic words. Which curriculum topic is this?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Module 2 · Lesson 12.</li><li>B. Module 1 · Lesson 3.</li></ul><ol><li>A learner starts with a Basic word and uses context to identify a narrower referent. Which curriculum topic is this?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Module 2 · Lesson 12.</li><li>B. Module 1 · Lesson 3.</li></ul><ol><li>A learner studies a source-recognized related meaning of an already-known Basic word. Is that the main task of C12?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes.</li><li>B. No. That belongs to the Module 1 meaning-expansion lessons.</li></ul><ol><li>The learner meets unless and needs to express the condition with if and not. Should this become another C12 section?</li></ol><ul><li>A. Yes. It is one of the seven C12 target words.</li><li>B. No. It remains in the conditional-sentence foundation.</li></ul><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>A — C12 moves from an ordinary-English non-Basic word to a contextual Basic paraphrase.</li><li>B — Module 1 · Lesson 3 moves from a Basic word to a narrower contextual referent.</li><li>B — Meaning expansion is a different task and belongs to Module 1 · Lessons 1–2.</li><li>B — The conditional relation remains in the existing foundation; it is not part of the seven-word C12 source set.</li></ol></div></details><h4>E. Reverse understanding</h4><p>For each Basic description, identify the source word and the context that it can represent. Do not give only the word; explain which sense is intended.</p><ol><li>The physical planet considered as a place in space.</li><li>People considered together as one group.</li><li>The changing state of society or circumstances.</li><li>The abstract sense of life.</li><li>People, animals or plants considered as entities that have life.</li><li>An -ing form used by itself as a Name for an activity.</li><li>A raised piece of land, described by its sloping form.</li><li>A whole region whose land contains many hills.</li><li>The actual place where a person lives.</li><li>A family-state context rather than a location.</li><li>The location identified for a person&#39;s private purposes.</li><li>The location connected with a business.</li><li>The heading written at the top of a letter.</li><li>A person with many tasks waiting to be done.</li><li>A person whose hearing is poor.</li></ol><details class=\"expr-answer\"><summary>查看答案与解析</summary><div class=\"expr-answer-body\"><ol><li>world — physical/planet sense.</li><li>world — people-as-a-group sense.</li><li>world — social conditions / changing circumstances.</li><li>life — the abstract sense.</li><li>life — living-object sense.</li><li>life — the activity sense, using the -ing word form.</li><li>hill — one raised landform.</li><li>hill / the regional hills context — describe the region, not one hill; do not use the source&#39;s regional term as a Basic entry.</li><li>home — residence sense.</li><li>home — family-state sense.</li><li>address — private address.</li><li>address — business address.</li><li>address — the heading written at the top of a letter.</li><li>busy — amount-of-work sense.</li><li>deaf — hearing condition.</li></ol></div></details><h4>F. Independent construction</h4><p>Complete all four tasks. First identify the intended sense, then construct a Basic description, and finally explain why another sense does not fit.</p><ol><li>Write two short contexts for world: one about the physical planet and one about people as a group. Give a different Basic description for each.</li><li>Write two contexts for life: one about its abstract sense and one about living objects. Explain why the same description cannot be used automatically in both.</li><li>Describe a hill as one landform, then describe a whole region containing many hills. Keep the two referents separate.</li><li>Write three contexts for address: private, business and the heading on a letter. Give a separate Basic description for each.</li></ol><p class=\"expr-boundary\"><strong>Checking boundary:</strong> these independent responses are not automatically scored by the current App. Do not claim that the current 850-word checker or the existing sentence checker evaluates arbitrary C12 paraphrases until a separate rule set has been designed and tested.</p><hr><h3>Lesson review</h3><p>You should now be able to:</p><ul><li>describe busy and deaf by identifying the condition in context;</li><li>distinguish the physical, collective-people and changing-conditions readings of world;</li><li>distinguish the abstract sense of life, living objects and the activity of living;</li><li>describe one hill separately from a whole region of hills;</li><li>distinguish home as a residence from a family-state context;</li><li>distinguish a private address, a business address and a letter heading;</li><li>use a context-first method instead of memorizing one replacement per ordinary-English word;</li><li>explain why C12 is different from Module 1 meaning expansion and meaning narrowing.</li></ul><p><strong>Module 2 is now drafted through Lesson 12.</strong> The next project task is a whole-course review of all 15 lesson drafts for duplicated examples, unclear boundaries, prerequisite gaps and inconsistent terminology. This does not change the accepted 2 Modules / 15 Lessons architecture.</p>"});})();
